blocking.why:
The basis allows a hang angle of up to 2 degrees. With a determinate cascade, any mismatch between the pick point and the centre of gravity makes the module hang tilted. Gravity then has a component along the floor, which has to be carried up through the end walls to the heads. R1-R7 are vertical only, and L1-L4 are self-cancelling sets at the heads, so no design case tests this load path.

I re-ran this independently on the committed code. Drop shares were fixed by the cascade, gravity and drop forces were resolved in the module's axes, and the support reactions came out at about 0:
- 2 degree roll: end-wall stud-base joints at 1.11 (W11 at x = 44, 3.94 kN on 3.54 kN) and 1.10 (W5). The wall-bottom-track tie carries 3.64 kN against a 3.54 kN T2 capacity.
- 1.5 degree roll: 0.84.
- 2 degree pitch: the NFT4 top chord reaches 1.00. This is partly a modelling artefact; it drops to 0.66 with the bridging modelled as a member.

The reviewer also found a 2.5 degree roll at 1.44. Still not included:
- wind on the lightest unit (about 0.7 degrees more);
- slew and tag-line loads;
- the plus or minus 10 % skew combined with tilt (R7d plus 2 degrees of roll gives 1.43 to 1.50, still over after adding end-wall screws);
- 10 to 17 more links that would then need ties.

blocking.fix:
Detail a head that physically cannot carry vertical load. Options:
- a lateral guide with running clearance;
- shouldered bolts or sleeves longer than the clamped thickness;
- slots longer than the strap stretch plus tolerance, with the gap checked before each lift.

Otherwise quantify the friction share and design the roof rim, track links and ties for it. Include the head in the H4 proof test, with the guide load measured.

blocking.why:
liftcase.py:99 (committed) sets the twist length to max(L_in, min(L_out, 1200)). DESIGN_BASIS section 5 defines only the in-plane and out-of-plane lengths, and 1200 mm matches no restraint position.

I re-ran the committed code with the twist length equal to the stated out-of-plane length. Four members fail:
- end roof-truss top chords NFT46: 1.61 and NFT26: 1.57;
- NFT4 top chord (the governing member): 1.20;
- W7 stud: 1.19.

With twist restrained at every joint, the maximum is 0.68. So the reported 0.91 cannot be derived from the stated basis. Lateral buckling in minor-axis bending with the lips in compression is not coded either. It is harmless at 1200 mm or less, but not at 2.7 to 3.1 m.

Every member is checked as a plain C89 with no holes. framed.json lists:
- 2396 lip notches, including 896 on top chords and 988 on bottom chords at the web joints;
- 2290 swages;
- 100 web notches;
- 59 service holes, including in W7-AC and W9-AC, the two most compressed studs.

A lip notch removes the flange's edge stiffener exactly at the chord panel points.

I made a cautious effective-width estimate at the design's own buckling stress (fn 159.5 MPa for NFT4). It gives about 11.7 kN at the notch against 13.2 kN in the governing NFT4 top chord. That is 1.13 from axial force alone; the plain section has 17.6 kN. The swaged web end in the notch helps, so the real loss is smaller, but no check covers it. If the twist restraint in the previous item is justified, the same estimate gives 0.87.

blocking.fix:
Choose one approach and state it in DESIGN_BASIS:
- adopt the Framer or roll-former test basis for notched chords, swaged-web joints and studs with holes;
- check the notched and holed sections (AS/NZS 4600 or AISI provisions for members with holes, or a finite-strip / DSM check of the notched section);
- forbid service holes in lift-critical members.

should_fix.fix:
Put a support rule on the lift drawing and the lift plan:
- continuous bearers or piers at 1.8 m or closer, levelled and packed;
- never on corners or the lift points alone;
- do not slacken the slings until every support is packed.

Add set-down cases to the analysis, specify packers between the cradles, and require the same supports for transport.

